WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.520
Claude can now turn simple text into animated videos and working dashboards. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 191. The Dakota reports that Anthropic, the company that builds the Claude Chatbot, added two beta features named Motion &amp; Dashboards. If Claude is new to you, it is a writing partner you message in a chat window, and it replies in plain language. Beta just means the features are still being tested, like a game you can play before the final version is locked in.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:33.440
Motion takes the words you type, plus any pictures you add, and builds a short clip

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:34.040
that moves.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.180
Dashboards reach into data sources and arrange that information as interactive charts and

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:39.920
live displays.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.040
It is like telling an artist what you want to see, then watching them sketch, animate, and

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:46.800
line the pictures into a clear story.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:51.600
The artist here is an artificial intelligence or AI, and it works in seconds.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:56.280
The tools now work on free accounts so you do not need a paid plan to experiment.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.960
That matters for school, because you can skip hours of learning a video editing app.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.200
Those apps can feel like you must learn every instrument before you are allowed to play

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:05.840
one song.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.900
With motion, you type what you want and get a finished animation in minutes.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.260
It also helps when numbers get messy.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.800
Whether you are tracking class grades or sports stats, you can ask for a live dashboard instead

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.200
of squinting at a grid of cells.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.320
A spreadsheet is like a long grocery list of numbers.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.100
A dashboard is like the scoreboard at a game, where those same numbers become something

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:28.180
you can read at a glance.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.300
For creative careers, this is AI acting as a co-creator rather than just a chatbot.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.020
A chatbot is like a vending machine for sentences.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.780
A co-creator is more like a bandmate who helps you shape the song.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.180
You might use it to explain a science concept for class or turn a story idea into a short

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:45.560
video.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:51.060
To try it, go to the Claude website, start a new chat on a free account, and type a request

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:57.620
in ordinary words. Type this, create an animated explainer about how rain forms, or type this,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.760
make a dashboard showing weekly study time. The system will generate the video or dashboard

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:08.120
right in the conversation. Okay, this one's a little tricky, but here is the simple version of how

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:13.660
a sentence becomes a moving clip. Describing a scene over the phone is ordinary, and the other person

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:18.860
starts to picture it. They might even sketch a quick version based only on your words. Now give

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:21.940
Give that person a memory stuffed with thousands of short video clips.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:26.840
They can pull pieces from those clips and combine them until the result matches what you described.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:32.520
The AI reads your text one small chunk at a time, not the whole paragraph in one gulp.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:37.580
It is a bit like reading a comic panel by panel, instead of glancing at the whole page.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.240
First it decides what should appear, then what should move next.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:46.340
Then it builds each frame by predicting the most likely next image that still fits the story.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:51.320
Predicting the next image is like autocomplete on your phone, except it is guessing the next

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:52.900
picture instead of the next word.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:54.820
It does that over and over.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:59.340
Along the way, it checks that the motion stays smooth and the objects stay consistent.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.160
Think of a paper flip book.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:05.900
Every page is a slightly different drawing, and when you flip them, the doodle seems to run.

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:11.160
Here, each new page is drawn to match the one before it, so a character does not suddenly

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:11.880
change shape.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:16.800
If you add an image as a starting point, the system uses that picture as the first frame.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.320
Then it generates the rest of the movement around that first picture, like continuing

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:22.700
a doodle someone else began.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:24.960
The result feels like a tiny movie.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.960
That is because the model has seen millions of real videos during training.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.760
Training is the long-study period where the AI looks at examples and learns patterns.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:38.880
It learned how things actually move in the world, the way you learn that a basketball bounces

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:39.980
and a feather drifts.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:44.560
That is why the animations can look natural even though nothing was filmed by a camera.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.760
Once you see it as a very fast flipbook, the trick sits in your hands, not in a cloud

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:50.280
of mystery.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:56.160
Meanwhile, someone put a whole creek in a browser, a Reddit post in a chat GPT community,

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:01.200
titled, A 3D Creek Running in Your Browser, shows a peaceful flowing creek scene.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:05.400
Chat GPT is another AI chatbot a lot of you have already tried.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:12.320
They built the scene using an AI model called GPT-6 Astra and a graphics toolkit called 3.js.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:17.380
3.js is a set of tools that helps a website draw three-dimensional scenes.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.180
So objects have depth and you can look around them.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:24.340
Walking around those objects is a bit like circling a sculpture in an art room,

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.240
except it is happening on a screen.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:29.700
Every rock, plant, and ripple was created in Blender,

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:33.900
which is a program artist used to sculpt digital objects like digital clay.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:38.780
the whole thing loads and moves smoothly inside a regular web browser with no

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:43.520
special software to install. It is a strong example of how AI can help generate

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.700
three-dimensional assets, the digital rocks and plants that used to take teams

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:52.820
of artists. If you enjoy games or world-building in places like Minecraft, this is a

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:56.920
peek at what current tools can already do. Find that Reddit post in the chat

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:03.880
GPT community, then tap the link in the post to load the scene yourself. Try

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:09.780
the water move. Separately, one student got tired of typing the same name, address, and work history

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:14.760
into dozens of job forms. That chore is like writing your name on the header of every worksheet

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:20.340
in a giant packet, even though your name never changes. They connected chat GPT to their email

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:26.200
and browser to scan listings and fill out matching applications. The AI compares job descriptions to

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:31.520
the resume, opens the forms, and fills the repeated fields automatically. It is not perfect,

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:39.720
Captures still stop at some times. A capture is one of those little puzzles that ask you to pick all the traffic lights to prove a human is at the keyboard.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.540
Think of it as a locked door, the automated helper cannot always open.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:46.600
Even so, it cuts down the boring parts dramatically.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:51.760
If you are applying for summer jobs or internships, that kind of workflow could save hours.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:57.300
Open chat GPT, upload your resume, and ask it to compare a job description you paste in.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:00.680
Then try the browser tool to see how it helps with the next steps.

